  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Mainly dry weather will continue through Saturday \nwith a few very minor exceptions.\n\nA longwave trough will remain in place over eastern Quebec and the \nCanadian Maritimes through Friday, with a series of shortwaves \npassing through the northwest flow aloft across northern NY and New \nEngland. Forcing and moisture associated with these features will \nremain far enough east to keep the majority of the area dry, with a \nfew minor exceptions.\n\nThrough early this evening, a few widely scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will develop east of Lake Ontario and across the Saint \nLawrence valley as a passing shortwave interacts with diurnal \ninstability, the lake breeze driven convergence zone, and terrain \ninduced boundaries. The best coverage will be across eastern \nJefferson and Lewis counties, but even here showers will remain \nscattered and uneven in nature. The showers will quickly end this \nevening with the loss of daytime heating. \n\nAnother shortwave will cross northern NY Friday afternoon, and may \nsupport a few isolated showers and thunderstorms across the Saint \nLawrence Valley, Adirondacks, and Central NY. Expect coverage of \nanything that develops to remain sparse given the very limited \nboundary layer moisture available. The trough will move away from \nthe area by Saturday, with high pressure surface and aloft providing \ndry weather areawide.\n\nLight winds and good radiational cooling will allow some patchy fog \nto develop late tonight through Friday morning, and again late \nFriday night through Saturday morning. The best chance of fog will \nbe in the typical areas across the Southern Tier river valleys, \nFinger Lakes, and North Country.\n\nKey Message 2...Showers and scattered thunderstorms are likely \nSunday.\n\nA mid level trough will advance east across the Great Lakes Sunday, \nwith an associated weak surface low passing over or just north of \nthe eastern Great Lakes. An area of quality moisture will advect \ninto the region just ahead of this system, with surface dewpoints \nclimbing into the upper 60s to lower 70s. Forcing for ascent from \nthe mid level shortwave and the warm front/cold front associated \nwith the surface low will support a few rounds of showers and \nscattered thunderstorms across the region Sunday through Sunday \nnight. The first round of showers may arrive as early as the pre-\ndawn hours of Sunday, with additional rounds Sunday through Sunday \nnight.\n\nWeak to moderate deep layer shear will be in place Sunday along the \nsouthern edge of the westerlies across the Great Lakes. Latest model \nguidance suggests an early arrival of clouds and showers Sunday, \nwhich may limit destabilization and severe weather potential.",
